WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:04.800
You know him as the Pumpkin King and&nbsp;
you definitely don't know him as Sandy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:07.920
Claws — but what do only adults&nbsp;
notice about Jack Skellington?

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.200
Jack Skellington is known as the Pumpkin King&nbsp;
— but what does that title actually mean? Jack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:18.240
certainly isn't a monarch, and Halloween Town&nbsp;
already has a leader: its two-faced mayor. Sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:23.220
Jack helps organize the spooking and scaring&nbsp;
that takes place on Halloween and gives some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:28.080
great speeches along the way, but it's tough&nbsp;
to say what exactly he does for an actual job.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:33.300
So he's a leader who holds no real political&nbsp;
power, he supposedly has a knack for motivating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:37.140
people, and he's a consultant to the&nbsp;
powers-that-be… yet it's hard to put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:41.460
your finger on what exactly he does for a&nbsp;
"real" job. It seems like Jack Skellington&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:46.560
is basically Halloween Town's version of the&nbsp;
British royal family. Jack truly is royalty as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.180
his title implies, and his status is complete&nbsp;
with the abject boredom that you'd expect from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.900
someone who exists to be a figurehead. No&nbsp;
wonder he's jealous of the hardworking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:58.500
much-credited Santa Claus. At least&nbsp;
Santa actually gets to do something.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:03.060
While Tim Burton wrote his delightfully&nbsp;
creepy poem, The Nightmare Before Christmas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:07.200
all the way back in 1982, the first&nbsp;
Hollywood feature film to truly nail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:11.940
the director's trademark spooky aesthetic&nbsp;
wouldn't come until 1988, with Beetlejuice.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:13.620
"It's showtime."

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:18.480
Many of the details in Beetlejuice are&nbsp;
similar to elements of The Nightmare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:23.100
Before Christmas. When Geena Davis and Alec&nbsp;
Baldwin are brought back in the séance scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.060
for example, their faces have the same&nbsp;
distinct, wrinkly lines seen on the faces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:31.200
of the dead in Halloween Town. Beetlejuice&nbsp;
uses games as a way to torture the people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:36.060
similar to Oogie Boogie's antagonism toward&nbsp;
Jack Skellington and Santa Claus. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:40.920
statues coming to life in Beetlejuice are echoed&nbsp;
by the kids' walking bathtub in Nightmare. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:45.120
even a ghostly hat decoration in Beetlejuice&nbsp;
that looks eerily similar to Jack Skellington.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:49.800
When Beetlejuice finally gets to have his fun&nbsp;
and haunt the humans, he appears wearing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:54.180
distinctive hat. At the top is a man with&nbsp;
a hollow-eyed skull in a familiar shape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:58.620
adorned by a bat that looks a lot like&nbsp;
Jack's bowtie. Whether this hat topper is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:03.660
based on Burton's idea for Jack Skellington or the&nbsp;
other way around, the resemblance is unmistakable.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:09.000
Jack's black-and-white suit looks like it's&nbsp;
glued on, and getting it on and off must be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.840
a straight-up nightmare. Moreover, when&nbsp;
Jack's Santa suit is destroyed when his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:16.800
sled is blown up by the military later&nbsp;
in the movie, it's revealed that he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.340
"wearing" his pinstripe suit under the&nbsp;
Santa suit the whole time. Therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:24.360
it would seem Jack never takes his suit&nbsp;
off — and we don't know if he actually can.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:28.620
If he can't, that would make sense for more&nbsp;
than just aesthetic reasons. Getting back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:32.400
to the fact that Jack is a skeleton, and&nbsp;
skeletons are held together by soft tissue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:36.120
that eventually rots away, you could argue&nbsp;
that he would fall apart without his suit.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:40.740
Obviously, there's already a lot of suspension of&nbsp;
disbelief that needs to occur in order to accept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:44.940
most of what we see in Halloween Town. Yet it&nbsp;
would make sense that Jack's suit also serves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:49.380
the purpose of keeping his bones together, just&nbsp;
like skin keeps our insides from falling out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:55.020
In a sense, Jack Skellington has black-and-white&nbsp;
pinstriped skin — like some sort of bipedal zebra.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:00.660
Why does Jack task Lock, Shock, and Barrel with&nbsp;
kidnapping the holiday icon he mistakenly refers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:05.580
to as "Sandy Claws"? Even more alarmingly, why&nbsp;
does he assume that the kids, also referred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:09.960
to as "Boogie's Boys," wouldn't go right to&nbsp;
Oogie Boogie and spill the beans? Seriously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:14.220
for a character who's so smart that the Mayor&nbsp;
needs his guidance on the yearly Halloween plans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:18.360
you've got to wonder: Why would Jack ever&nbsp;
trust these impish trick-or-treaters?

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:22.260
For one thing, they're not the sharpest tools&nbsp;
in the shed, since they accidentally kidnap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:26.520
the Easter Bunny instead of Santa. Secondly,&nbsp;
everybody in Halloween Town loves Jack and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:30.720
holds him in the highest esteem: Surely he&nbsp;
could have found an eager and loyal helper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:34.980
anywhere else? So why would he instead&nbsp;
pick three kids who, at best, treat him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:39.900
with amused disdain? It's not like Jack doesn't&nbsp;
know about their association with Oogie Boogie.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:46.440
"And one more thing… leave that no&nbsp;
account Oogie Boogie out of this!"

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:50.400
So he knows where their true loyalties&nbsp;
lie but decides to count on them anyway.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.240
It seems that Jack isn't as clever as&nbsp;
the residents of Halloween Town think.

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:59.520
As he sings the number "Jack's Lament," Jack&nbsp;
removes his head, holding it out like Hamlet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:04.500
holds the exhumed jester Yorick's skull while&nbsp;
reciting his famous "Alas, poor Yorick" speech.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:08.340
The fact that Jack is a skeleton, and can&nbsp;
therefore bend the rules of his anatomy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:12.840
allows him to embody both Yorick and Hamlet&nbsp;
in the recreation of this Shakespearean scene.

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:17.340
While this self-decapitation lasts just for&nbsp;
a moment during Jack's sweeping song about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:22.500
identity and futility, it speaks very strongly&nbsp;
to who Jack really is. One part of him is Hamlet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:27.420
full of ennui and self-doubt, wondering at the&nbsp;
purpose of his existence. But another part of him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:32.700
is the jester Yorick, the silly fool who can make&nbsp;
you laugh and cry, who can tumble and fall and get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:37.680
back up with a big smile. In this one moment,&nbsp;
Jack shows just how complicated he truly is.

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:43.740
In animation, nearly every detail on screen is&nbsp;
drawn on purpose, and this goes especially for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:48.480
a movie produced by a man known for crafting&nbsp;
a very specific, detailed aesthetic style. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:52.800
The Nightmare Before Christmas, Jack Skellington's&nbsp;
hands show the joints between his knuckles, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:57.900
makes sense, as this emphatic leader is big on&nbsp;
hand gestures and physical articulation. But if he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:03.420
needs joints to move his fingers, how does he move&nbsp;
his neck? That looks like one single fused bone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:08.100
rather than a stack of vertebrae. Sure, it has&nbsp;
wrinkles, but there don't appear to be any joints.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:11.760
Based simply on how skeletons work,&nbsp;
Jack's neck couldn't bend, twist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:15.720
and turn without vertebrae — after all,&nbsp;
bones don't move and bend themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:19.200
but instead achieve mobility through the&nbsp;
combined efforts of their muscles and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:23.880
joints. How does the ever-introspective Jack&nbsp;
bend his neck in contemplation and wonderment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:28.320
then? Such deep questions, hard-hitting&nbsp;
and poignant, may never be answered.

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:32.760
Disney had some serious apprehensions about&nbsp;
producing The Nightmare Before Christmas as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:37.620
children's movie. Their concerns about the horror&nbsp;
elements led Disney to release the film under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:41.700
company's "grown-up" subsidiary, Touchstone&nbsp;
Pictures, instead of the main Disney label.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:45.000
"How horrible our Christmas will be."

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:48.300
"No, how jolly!"

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:53.160
The entertainment giant also requested&nbsp;
a few changes. For example, Disney execs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:56.040
demanded that Jack Skellington&nbsp;
should have eyes in his sockets.

00:05:56.040 --> 00:06:01.020
They say the eyes are the windows to the soul, a&nbsp;
fountain of emotion that allows people to relate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:05.100
to each other — so perhaps Disney was worried&nbsp;
that Jack would seem lacking in personality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:09.360
without them. Or maybe the studio was worried&nbsp;
that Jack Skellington would look too sinister&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:14.760
for younger audiences. Luckily, producer-creator&nbsp;
Tim Burton and director Henry Selick stood their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:18.960
ground on the point that Jack is, in fact,&nbsp;
a skeleton, and skeletons don't have eyes.

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:22.560
It's a good thing they stood strong too,&nbsp;
because Jack, now a beloved character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:27.300
doesn't seem to have suffered any kind of hit for&nbsp;
having empty eye sockets. In fact, his face is so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:31.680
expressive that kids may not even notice that&nbsp;
he has no eyes — even if Disney definitely did.

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:37.020
Of course, people emote with more than just their&nbsp;
eyes. For example, a fair bit of emotion comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:41.460
across in the eyebrows and the mouth. Without&nbsp;
eyes, Jack has to depend even more on these kinds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:45.960
of facial reactions. The only problem with this,&nbsp;
of course, is that skulls can't actually move.

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:50.880
Muscles and skin create the appearance of brows&nbsp;
going up or down, as well as contorting the lips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:56.460
into a smile or a grimace. The human skull doesn't&nbsp;
move in real life, since bone is solid. Certainly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.460 --> 00:07:00.720
there has to be some artistic license at play&nbsp;
to give a skeleton without eyes some semblance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:06.060
of emotion, and, to that end, the animators afford&nbsp;
Jack's skeletal face the same flexibility he would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:10.440
have if it were covered in skin. It's almost&nbsp;
as if Jack Skellington's skull is made of clay.

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:15.240
There are some weird things going on with&nbsp;
Jack Skellington, anatomically speaking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:19.320
One aspect of his body that draws a critical&nbsp;
eye is that his mouth appears to be stitched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:23.700
together… except when it's not. The lines&nbsp;
running up and down Jack's face could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:29.100
seen simply as stylized wrinkly lips at first,&nbsp;
like the remnants of flesh in a state of decay.

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:31.320
But if you look toward the back of Jack's skull,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:35.400
his mouth looks like it's just sewn together.&nbsp;
When you think about it, this makes sense:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:39.660
There are no muscles to hold the bottom&nbsp;
and top of his jaw in place. When closed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:43.920
his mouth looks like it's strung together. When&nbsp;
open, it's as if the threads have magically split,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:48.060
except towards the back, where they hold the&nbsp;
hinges of the jaw together. So you can probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:52.560
add another unique feature to Jack's anatomy:&nbsp;
Apparently, string can be sewn through his bone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:56.520
to take the place of muscles and tendons.&nbsp;
Jack Skellington is truly a medical marvel.

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:01.800
Jack Skellington is a collection of bones without&nbsp;
connective or protective tissue, held together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:06.960
in large part by a suit, possibly with stitches&nbsp;
securing his jaws. You wouldn't think that makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:12.000
for a particularly durable body. Yet Jack gets&nbsp;
hammered by cannon fire and escapes with little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:17.100
more than a burned Santa suit. At another point,&nbsp;
he leaps around Oogie's projectiles and traps like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:22.140
a bona fide gymnast. And even if Jack had been&nbsp;
hit, he probably would have come out feeling fine.

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:25.080
Take the movie's final act, for&nbsp;
example. The Pumpkin King is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:28.740
blasted out of the sky by the military&nbsp;
and springs back up almost immediately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:32.940
even retaining enough strength and power&nbsp;
to return home and save Santa Claus and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:36.600
Sally. Perhaps there's some truth to&nbsp;
the idea that you can't die twice.

